<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageId>PicoLog</PackageId>
    <Description>High-performance structured logging with compile-time message templates and built-in sinks.</Description>
    <PackageTags>PicoHex;Logging;AOT</PackageTags>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="PicoLog.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(UseProjectReferences)' == 'true'">
    <ProjectReference Include="..\PicoLog.Abs\PicoLog.Abs.csproj" PrivateAssets="Build" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(UseProjectReferences)' != 'true'">
    <PackageReference Include="PicoLog.Abs" PrivateAssets="Build" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The other half of the "PicoLog carries no serializer" pin. AssemblySurfaceTests.
    PicoLog_ReferencesNoSerializer checks the ASSEMBLY's references, so it sees a serializer that
    is USED — including one that ships inside the shared framework (System.Text.Json lives in
    Microsoft.NETCore.App, so `using System.Text.Json` needs no PackageReference at all). What it
    cannot see is an UNUSED <PackageReference>: Roslyn emits an AssemblyRef only for assemblies
    whose types are used, so a bare `<PackageReference Include="PicoJetson" />` leaves PicoLog.dll's
    metadata unchanged and that pin green (measured: its 194 tests still pass against exactly that
    build) — while every consumer of PicoLog still inherits the package. The project file is the
    only place that half is visible.

    The allow-list is exactly PicoLog.Abs: core's entire dependency list is PicoLog.Abs plus
    in-framework assemblies. Serialization is PicoLog.Json's job — the sinks that serialize
    (JsonLogSink, SeqSink) ship there, with PicoJetson.

    Only PackageReferences DECLARED IN THIS FILE count (%(DefiningProjectFullPath)), so the
    inherited Microsoft.SourceLink.GitHub (Directory.Build.props), the SDK's own
    Microsoft.DotNet.ILCompiler / Microsoft.NET.ILLink.Tasks injection
    (Microsoft.NET.Sdk.FrameworkReferenceResolution.targets) and the ProjectReference used when
    UseProjectReferences=true neither satisfy nor trip the guard. Both UseProjectReferences
    branches validate: the ProjectReference branch has no PackageReference at all, the other has
    the single allowed one, and an empty list is a pass.

    Timing: Build and GenerateNuspec. Restore never runs it, so a first-time restore is unaffected.

    GenerateNuspec, not Pack: MSBuild runs a target's DependsOnTargets before any target hooked
    via BeforeTargets, and Pack depends on GenerateNuspec (which itself depends on Build unless
    NoBuild is set). Hooking Pack therefore fired the guard AFTER the package had been written.
    Measured on this worktree with the offending reference present: a no-build pack
    (`dotnet pack -p:NoBuild=true`) printed "Successfully created package
    ...PicoLog.0.0.0-dev.nupkg" ABOVE the guard error, and that .nupkg really listed
    `<dependency id="PicoJetson" version="2026.13.0" exclude="Build,Analyzers" />`; the exit
    code was 1, so CI still failed and no push step ran, but a pipeline that collects
    bin/**/*.nupkg regardless of exit status would have carried the package. GenerateNuspec is
    the last target that runs before the file is written, so failing there leaves no .nupkg on
    disk. Build is kept so a plain `build` fails too, and because a plain `pack` builds first: the
    guard then fires at Build, before nuspec generation is even reached.
  -->
  <Target Name="PicoLogCoreDeclaresNoSerializer" BeforeTargets="Build;GenerateNuspec">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_PicoLogCoreOwnPackageReference
        Include="@(PackageReference)"
        Condition="'%(PackageReference.DefiningProjectFullPath)' == '$(MSBuildProjectFullPath)'"
      />
      <_PicoLogCoreForeignPackageReference
        Include="@(_PicoLogCoreOwnPackageReference)"
        Exclude="PicoLog.Abs"
      />
    </ItemGroup>
    <Error
      Condition="'@(_PicoLogCoreForeignPackageReference)' != ''"
      Text="PicoLog (core) may declare exactly one package dependency: PicoLog.Abs. Offending PackageReference(s): @(_PicoLogCoreForeignPackageReference, ', ').&#10;&#10;Serialization is PicoLog.Json's job: the sinks that serialize (JsonLogSink, SeqSink) ship in that package, together with PicoJetson. Core's entire dependency list is PicoLog.Abs plus in-framework assemblies — a package declared here is inherited by every consumer of PicoLog. If a sink needs it, that sink belongs in PicoLog.Json.&#10;&#10;This guard covers the UNUSED reference, which leaves PicoLog.dll's metadata untouched; a serializer that is actually USED additionally trips AssemblySurfaceTests.PicoLog_ReferencesNoSerializer."
    />
  </Target>
</Project>
